=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nftables `FORWARD` chain on `router6`, applied to inbound traffic on `eth2` (the `r6_r3` uplink connecting to router3 and the wider backbone).

**Mechanism:** `router6` has four rules in its FORWARD chain all matching `iifname "eth2"`. After one narrow ACCEPT (TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1), three catch-all DROP rules are applied: one dropping all TCP, one dropping all UDP, and one dropping everything else (including ICMP). This means every transit packet — pings, data flows, routing probes — arriving at router6 from the router3 side is silently discarded before reaching the 172.18.51.0/24 (zone3) subnet. Live counters confirmed 28 actively dropped packets at time of inspection.

**Localization:** `router6`, interface `eth2` (collision domain `r6_r3`, router3.eth2 ↔ router6.eth2, subnet 10.50.1.0/24), FORWARD chain in the `ip filter` table.

**Root cause:** An ACL installed on router6's FORWARD hook drops all transit traffic entering via the backbone-facing interface `eth2`, silently blackholing all inter-zone traffic destined for zone3 (172.18.51.0/24 and 172.18.50.0/24). The `wan_link_r0_r1` link is physically UP on both sides and is not involved. All other physical links and routing tables (RIP + kernel statics) are intact and consistent.

**Fix:** Flush the three DROP rules from `router6`'s nftables FORWARD chain on `iifname "eth2"` (e.g., `nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD` or selectively delete the DROP rules), restoring normal packet forwarding through the router3→router6 backbone path into zone3.
=== END ===